{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Narrowed two of the snapshot question's remaining variants by source and corrected one of my earlier counter-arguments, without another execution. Correction: I had written that the StopSign adjustment of the equal case might keep ordinary requests inside the entries; measured against the observed state it does not — a request equal to the drifted decided index is mapped to the decided log without the StopSign, which is 8 rather than 9, and 8 still exceeds the content of six indices — so the equal case is not a protection here. Narrowings: the bound is computed from the decided index of the node that receives the call, so a follower whose own decided index has drifted the same way would accept the same request, and local_only only decides whether the compaction is forwarded to the peers; and the form the loss takes is whatever the entry type's Snapshot::create does with an empty slice, which here is an empty snapshot that the reads then report for the compacted region. The account keeps conclusion violated with defect_claim asserted on the same receipt (e70b8baa087246c78ea3a64f24b8e97c events 0-5), keeps the counterevidence that the call returned Ok, that the requested index was inside the documented allowance, and that the equal-case protection does not hold, and lists the limits: the entry-type dependence, the peers sampled only through their accepted and decided indexes, the two variants argued from the derivation rather than executed, and the shared-name admission caveat behind the unknown comparison outcomes. No execution was requested this turn; incomplete are the follower-side and equal-index variants as executions, the trim child's storage-side remainder, the parent drift's bound and further-producer questions, timers and loss triggers, larger clusters, persistence, and any further independent lead.\"}"}}
